  {caption && <figcaption>A head on a 256-dimensional embedding, trained on n labels. Below about two labels per dimension a separating hyperplane almost always exists (Cover, 1965), and an unpenalised fit finds it, drives the weights to infinity and slams every probability into 0 or 1. The hyperplane is always there. The probability is the penalty&#x27;s doing.</figcaption>}
